Setup Requirements
Ensure you have at least 2 non-root accounts created:
sudo useradd user1
sudo useradd user2
sudo passwd user1
sudo passwd user2
Lab Tasks
Task 1: Log in as different users using su and sudo
Subtask 1.1: Switch users with su
Open a terminal on your Linux system

Switch to user1:

su - user1
Verify your current user:

whoami
Expected Output: user1

Return to your original user:

exit
Subtask 1.2: Execute commands with sudo
As a regular user, try viewing the /etc/shadow file:

cat /etc/shadow
Expected Outcome: Permission denied error

Now try with sudo:

sudo cat /etc/shadow
Expected Outcome: If you have sudo privileges, you'll see the file contents

Check your sudo privileges:

sudo -l
Troubleshooting Tip: If you get "user is not in the sudoers file", your account needs to be added to the sudo group:

sudo usermod -aG sudo yourusername
Task 2: Switch between users and understand permissions
Subtask 2.1: Create and test file access
As user1, create a file:

su - user1
touch ~/user1_file.txt
echo "This is user1's file" > ~/user1_file.txt
As user2, try to access the file:

su - user2
cat /home/user1/user1_file.txt
Expected Outcome: Permission denied

Examine the file permissions:

ls -l /home/user1/user1_file.txt
Subtask 2.2: Modify file permissions
As user1, change the file permissions:

su - user1
chmod 644 ~/user1_file.txt
As user2, try accessing again:

su - user2
cat /home/user1/user1_file.txt
Expected Outcome: Now you can read the file

Key Concept: Linux file permissions (read=4, write=2, execute=1) control access for owner, group, and others.

Task 3: Configure multiuser targets
Subtask 3.1: Check current target
View the current system target:
systemctl get-default
Common Outputs:
graphical.target (GUI mode)
multi-user.target (text mode)
Subtask 3.2: Change between targets
Switch to multi-user (text) target:

sudo systemctl isolate multi-user.target
Switch back to graphical target:

sudo systemctl isolate graphical.target
Set the default target (persistent across reboots):

sudo systemctl set-default multi-user.target
Troubleshooting Tip: If you lose GUI access, you can switch back from a text console with:

sudo systemctl start gdm  # or lightdm, depending on your display manager
Advanced Task: Container User Management with Podman
Run a container as a specific user:

podman run --user 1000 -it fedora /bin/bash
Check the user inside the container:

whoami
Expected Output: You'll see you're user with UID 1000

Exit the container:

exit
